{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "growth_lr2m_D_bin_closed",
    "description": "Integrated divergence, over each SECTION of a sectional size distribution, of the condensational-growth flux of particle NUMBER, int_section d(G n)/dv dv, for the growth law G(v) = lam v (each section's particles grow at that section's uniform relative rate lam, the volume-proportional growth of a sectional model in which the condensed volume of a section is shared over its particles in proportion to their volume). The consumer writes the number-moment equation as dN/dt = -D(lam*N, V, wrt: bin) + ..., where N [cell, bin] is the section NUMBER and the trailing operand V [cell, bin] (esm-spec 9.6.8 'trailing operands on D') is the section dry VOLUME moment the reconstruction reads; lam [cell, bin] is 1/V dV/dt of the section's own condensation (zero for a section the consumer considers inactive). The lowered value per section is outflow through both edges minus inflow from the neighbours, telescoping, so sum_j dN_j/dt = 0 to round-off. CLOSED size range: nothing grows out of the top section nor shrinks out of the bottom one (the first and last regions drop the outer edge fluxes), so particles growing past vb[NBIN+1] stay in section NBIN and shrinking ones below vb[1] stay in section 1, as in MOSAIC. RECONSTRUCTION AND EDGE FLUX. Within each section the number density is linear in v on a support [lo, hi] inside the section, fixed by the two moments (N, V) -- MOSAIC's 'linear-discrete' / mass-number-advection reconstruction (move_sections_calc_masnumadvect): the full section when the mean volume vbar = V/N lies in the middle third, a triangle that vanishes at the far end when it lies in the lower or upper third (sectional_lr2m_support_top / _bottom), and no reconstruction (zero edge flux) when vbar lies within 1 % of the section's width of either edge (sectional_lr2m_interior_mean). The edge flux is upwind: the growth flux G n at an edge is evaluated in the section that grows into it (lam > 0 sends particles up through the upper edge) or shrinks into it (lam < 0 sends them down through the lower edge), G(v_edge) n(v_edge) = |lam| v_edge n_edge (sectional_lr2m_number_flux_up / _down). WHY THIS IS THE RIGHT OPERATOR. MOSAIC's movesect is fully discrete in time: after a step's growth it maps every particle of section j from v to s v (s = vbar_after/vbar_before, the section's volume growth factor) and integrates the linear density over the pulled-back section edges. As the growth step goes to zero (s = 1 + lam dt), the fraction of number carried across the upper edge is (1 + b/2) N (vb[j+1] - vb[j+1]/s)/(hi - lo) = lam dt vb[j+1] n_top, and that limit is this flux; verified against a real64 replay of WRF's own move_sections at growth fractions 1e-6 and 2e-6 of a recorded 60 s step (EqWeFiC data/eqwefic/notes/chem170_mosaic_movesect_capture.md). MOSAIC's finite-step relocations ('moving center': a section whose mean has left it is moved whole; and the number conform that clips a section's mean back inside its edges) are projections, not rates, and are not part of this operator. First order. Requires NBIN >= 2.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:rule",
      "family:finite_volume",
      "grid:sectional_volume_1d",
      "op:advection",
      "order:1",
      "scheme:linear-discrete",
      "scheme:upwind",
      "bc:closed",
      "form:flux",
      "spec:9.6.8",
      "axes:cell,bin"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Zaveri, R. A., Easter, R. C., Fast, J. D., & Peters, L. K. (2008). Model for Simulating Aerosol Interactions and Chemistry (MOSAIC). Journal of Geophysical Research, 113, D13204.",
        "doi": "10.1029/2007JD008782"
      },
      {
        "citation": "WRF-Chem v4.8.0 chem/module_mosaic_movesect.F, subroutines move_sections_calc_masnumadvect and move_sections_apply_moves (R. C. Easter), the 'linear-discrete' / mass-number-advection section-moving algorithm (msectional = 20)."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Gelbard, F., Tambour, Y., & Seinfeld, J. H. (1980). Sectional representations for simulating aerosol dynamics. Journal of Colloid and Interface Science, 76(2), 541-556.",
        "doi": "10.1016/0021-9797(80)90394-X"
      }
    ]
  },
  "expression_template_imports": [
    {
      "ref": "../stencils/sectional_lr2m_growth_number_interior.esm"
